{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CfbZZaai9kCQttYijGws5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When you squeeze the brake lever on the handlebars, you pull a cable that runs down the frame toward the wheel. This cable is under tension and transmits your hand's force mechanically to the brake mechanism. The harder you squeeze the lever, the more the cable tightens, creating greater stopping power.

The cable pulls a metal arm that forces two rubber brake pads against the sides of the wheel's rim. As the pads press against the spinning rim, friction is generated between them. This friction slows the wheel's rotation, bringing the bicycle to a stop. Releasing the lever allows a spring to push the pads away from the rim, letting the wheel spin freely again."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":157,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null}

